## What this repository derives

- `data/labeled-all.jsonl` — a document-kind label for each Harvey document
- `data/index.json` — an index of Harvey documents by id, version and size
- `out/classifications.{csv,json}`, `out/needs-review.csv` — the classifications

These are derived data keyed to Harvey documents. They contain no Harvey
document text. To use them you need the corpus itself, from the repository
above (or, in this project's case, via the `legal-ai-mvp` checkout that
imports it).

The 29 document kinds in `taxonomy.json` are our own, written from legal domain
knowledge and then checked against what the corpus actually contains. They are
not Harvey's taxonomy and Harvey does not endorse them.

## Provenance, and what it does not mean

Harvey states, in `docs/tutorial.md` of the source repository:

> Documents across the benchmark are synthetically generated in large batches,
> under the guidance and review of human lawyers. While they represent real
> world legal work in substantive complexity, they contain imperfections and
> should not be taken as perfectly reflecting documents drafted from scratch by
> a practising lawyer.

That is meaningful provenance and Harvey is admirably clear about its limits.
It is **not** a per-document sign-off, and Harvey does not claim it is. Known
defects exist in the corpus — for example, several engagement letters cite a
matter number belonging to a different client's matter.

**Treat this corpus, and any classification of it produced here, as realistic
demonstration material. Never as a model of correct legal drafting, and never
as legal advice.**

